\begin_layout Standard
Controlling the onboard LED on the Pico 2 W via a task is significantly more complex than standard GPIO toggling.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the wireless board variant the green LED is not connected to the RP2350 microcontroller directly but is wired to the WL_GPIO0 pin on the Infineon CYW43439 wireless chip.
 [1,
 2,
 3] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To turn it on or off from an Ada task,
 your program must orchestrate a low-level SPI and bit-banging communication sequence to send packets over to the external chip.
 [2,
 4] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Architecture:
 How the RP2350 Talks to the LED
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To change the state of the onboard LED four distinct pins on your RP2350 must be dynamically coordinated:
 [1,
 5] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GPIO 23:
 Power control / Reset to the Infineon chip.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GPIO 25:
 The chip select (CS) line to initiate communication.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GPIO 29:
 The shared SPI clock (CLK) line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GPIO 24:
 The bi-directional SPI Data line (DIO/IRQ).
 [1,
 5]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because this involves state tracking,
 utilizing an Ada Protected Object is the best architectural pattern to prevent multiple tasks from trying to access the shared SPI communication wires at the same time.
 [6] 
\end_layout

