\begin_layout Standard
 Important Practical Tradeoffs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Writing a bare-metal custom driver in Ada to manually parse the [CYW43439 SPI control structures](https://pip.raspberrypi.com/documents/RP-008304-DS-pico-2-w-datasheet.pdf) requires substantial register-level mapping.
 [1,
 4] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If your goal is building a large-scale project rather than writing educational,
 raw registers,
 the alternative path is importing the pre-built C-based Pico SDK drivers (pico_cyw43_arch) directly into your Ada project.
 You can do this by using Ada's built-in Foreign Function Interface (FFI):
 [8] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
-- Interface to import the pre-written driver library via C linkage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
procedure cyw43_arch_gpio_put (Pin :
 Interfaces.C.int;
 Value :
 Interfaces.C.int)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with Import => True,
 Convention => C,
 External_Name => "cyw43_arch_gpio_put";
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Would you like to continue building out the raw,
 register-level bit-bang sequence inside your Ada protected object,
 or should we set up the Ada-to-C FFI bindings to leverage the official Pico SDK drivers instead?
 [8] 
\end_layout

